Wazuh SOC/SIEM Laboratory — Technical Documentation
1. Introduction

This project consists of implementing a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) laboratory using Wazuh, an open-source security platform designed for endpoint monitoring, security event analysis, threat detection, File Integrity Monitoring (FIM), vulnerability detection, Security Configuration Assessment (SCA), and security event investigation.

The main objective of the laboratory was to reproduce, in a virtualized environment, part of the operation of a Security Operations Center (SOC).

The laboratory consisted of three main virtual machines:

Ubuntu Server 24.04 — Wazuh central server;
Windows 10 — monitored Windows endpoint;
Kali Linux — monitored Linux endpoint and controlled security-testing machine.

The Wazuh architecture is composed primarily of the Wazuh Server, Wazuh Indexer, and Wazuh Dashboard, while the monitored endpoints use the Wazuh Agent. The agents collect security-related information and forward it to the Wazuh Server for analysis and processing. The resulting data is stored in the Indexer and presented through the Dashboard.

2. Project Objectives

The main objectives of the laboratory were:

Deploy a functional Wazuh SIEM environment;
Configure a centralized Wazuh server;
Connect Windows and Linux endpoints;
Collect security events and logs;
Monitor endpoint activity;
Configure Sysmon on Windows;
Configure Auditd on Linux;
Implement File Integrity Monitoring;
Monitor endpoint vulnerabilities;
Investigate security events;
Associate events with MITRE ATT&CK techniques;
Generate controlled security events;
Analyze alerts through the Wazuh Dashboard;
Practice SOC investigation and troubleshooting procedures.

The project was also designed as a practical cybersecurity portfolio project that could later be documented and published on GitHub.

3. Laboratory Architecture

The laboratory was built using virtual machines.

The architecture followed an all-in-one Wazuh deployment, which is appropriate for laboratory environments and small deployments.

The central Wazuh components were installed on the Ubuntu Server:

Wazuh Server;
Wazuh Indexer;
Wazuh Dashboard.

The endpoints had the Wazuh Agent installed.

The resulting architecture was:

                    ┌─────────────────────────┐
                    │     Wazuh Dashboard     │
                    │      Web Interface      │
                    └────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │
                                 ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────┐
                    │      Wazuh Indexer      │
                    │   Storage and Search    │
                    └────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │
                                 ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────┐
                    │      Wazuh Server       │
                    │    Analysis / Manager   │
                    └────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │
                   ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
                   │                           │
                   ▼                           ▼
          ┌─────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────┐
          │    Windows 10   │         │    Kali Linux   │
          │  Wazuh Agent    │         │  Wazuh Agent    │
          │     + Sysmon    │         │     + Auditd    │
          └─────────────────┘         └─────────────────┘

The overall data flow was:

Endpoint → Wazuh Agent → Wazuh Server → Wazuh Indexer → Wazuh Dashboard

4. Virtualization Environment

The entire laboratory was deployed using virtual machines.

The host system provided approximately:

16 CPUs
24 GB of RAM

Resources were distributed between the Wazuh Server, Windows 10, and Kali Linux virtual machines.

This allowed the project to simulate a small enterprise security environment in which a centralized security platform monitors multiple endpoints.

5. Ubuntu Server Installation

The first system configured was Ubuntu Server 24.04.

Ubuntu Server was selected as the central Wazuh server because it is supported by Wazuh and is widely used for server and infrastructure environments.

After installing the operating system, the initial configuration included:

Updating system packages;
Configuring the network;
Checking network connectivity;
Configuring the hostname;
Installing required utilities;
Verifying the server IP address.

Network connectivity between the Ubuntu Server and the other virtual machines was also verified.

The VirtualBox network configuration was particularly important because the endpoints needed to communicate with the Wazuh Server.

6. Wazuh Installation

Once Ubuntu Server was prepared, Wazuh was installed using the official deployment method.

The main components installed were:

Wazuh Server

The Wazuh Server is responsible for receiving and analyzing data from the agents.

It performs tasks such as:

Receiving events;
Decoding logs;
Applying detection rules;
Generating alerts;
Processing security data;
Managing agents.
Wazuh Indexer

The Wazuh Indexer is responsible for storing and indexing the data processed by the Wazuh Server.

This allows security events and alerts to be searched and analyzed later.

Wazuh Dashboard

The Dashboard provides the graphical interface used to interact with the platform.

It allows analysts to:

Monitor agents;
View alerts;
Search events;
Investigate incidents;
Review vulnerabilities;
Analyze FIM events;
Review MITRE ATT&CK information;
Monitor endpoint information.
7. Network Communication

Understanding network communication was an important part of the implementation.

Some of the main Wazuh services use the following ports:

Port	Purpose
1514/TCP	Agent communication
1515/TCP	Agent enrollment
55000/TCP	Wazuh API
9200/TCP	Wazuh Indexer API
443/TCP	Wazuh Dashboard

The Wazuh Agent uses the Wazuh Server to establish communication and forward security information.

Therefore, network connectivity had to be verified before troubleshooting the agents themselves.

8. Wazuh Dashboard

After installing the Wazuh components, the Dashboard was accessed through a web browser.

The Dashboard became the main interface for interacting with the laboratory.

From the Dashboard, it was possible to access:

Agent information;
Security alerts;
Events;
Severity levels;
Vulnerabilities;
File Integrity Monitoring;
MITRE ATT&CK information;
Endpoint information;
Inventory data.

This provided a centralized view of the security state of the laboratory.

9. Windows 10 Endpoint Configuration

The next step was configuring Windows 10 as a monitored endpoint.

The Wazuh Agent was installed on Windows 10 and configured to communicate with the Wazuh Server.

The basic communication flow became:

Windows 10
     │
     ▼
Wazuh Agent
     │
     ▼
Wazuh Server

After installation, the agent was registered with the Wazuh Server.

The Dashboard was then used to verify whether the Windows endpoint appeared and whether its connection status was active.

10. Windows Agent Troubleshooting

During the Windows configuration, we encountered problems with the agent not appearing correctly in the Wazuh Dashboard.

We investigated several possible causes, including:

Whether the Wazuh Agent service was running;
Whether the agent configuration was correct;
Whether the Wazuh Server IP address was correct;
Whether the Windows machine could reach the server;
Whether the agent enrollment process had been completed;
Whether administrative permissions were available.

This troubleshooting process was important because it demonstrated a real-world infrastructure security scenario.

After correcting the configuration, communication between Windows and the Wazuh Server was successfully established.

11. Sysmon Installation

After successfully configuring the Windows agent, Microsoft Sysmon (System Monitor) was introduced.

Sysmon is part of the Microsoft Sysinternals suite and provides detailed telemetry about system activity.

It can provide information related to activities such as:

Process creation;
Network connections;
File-related activity;
Process-related events;
Other system activities relevant to security investigations.

The purpose of using Sysmon was to provide richer telemetry to the Wazuh platform.

The resulting flow was:

Windows Activity
       ↓
     Sysmon
       ↓
Windows Event Log
       ↓
Wazuh Agent
       ↓
Wazuh Server
       ↓
Wazuh Indexer
       ↓
Wazuh Dashboard

This significantly increased the amount of useful security telemetry available from the Windows endpoint.

12. Wazuh and Sysmon Integration

After installing Sysmon, the Wazuh Agent was configured to collect the events generated by Sysmon through the Windows Event Log.

This allowed Sysmon telemetry to be forwarded to the Wazuh Server.

The purpose was not simply to collect raw logs, but to allow the Wazuh detection engine to process those events and generate useful security information.

This created a more realistic endpoint-monitoring environment.

13. Kali Linux Configuration

The second monitored endpoint was Kali Linux.

Kali Linux served two purposes:

It was a monitored Linux endpoint.
It was used to perform controlled security tests against the laboratory.

The Wazuh Agent was installed and configured on Kali Linux.

The resulting configuration was:

Kali Linux
   │
   ├── Wazuh Agent
   │
   ├── Auditd
   │
   └── Security Testing Tools
            │
            ▼
       Wazuh Server

This allowed the Wazuh Server to receive telemetry from both Windows and Linux systems.

14. Auditd Configuration

Auditd, part of the Linux Audit Framework, was configured on Kali Linux.

Auditd provides detailed auditing information about activities occurring within the Linux operating system.

The objective was to collect these audit events through the Wazuh Agent.

The resulting flow was:

Activity on Kali Linux
        ↓
      Auditd
        ↓
 Audit Log Events
        ↓
   Wazuh Agent
        ↓
   Wazuh Server
        ↓
 Wazuh Dashboard

One of the validation steps performed during the project was confirming that Auditd events were successfully reaching the Wazuh Dashboard.

15. Auditd Troubleshooting

During the Linux configuration, we specifically verified whether Auditd events were being correctly collected.

Installing Auditd alone was not sufficient.

The entire data path had to be verified:

Auditd
   ↓
Audit Logs
   ↓
Wazuh Agent
   ↓
Wazuh Server
   ↓
Wazuh Indexer
   ↓
Wazuh Dashboard

This helped demonstrate an important SIEM troubleshooting principle:

If an event does not appear in the SIEM, the problem may exist at the data source, collection, transport, processing, storage, or visualization layer.

16. File Integrity Monitoring

Another important Wazuh feature explored in the project was File Integrity Monitoring (FIM).

FIM is used to monitor selected files and detect unauthorized or unexpected modifications.

The general process is:

Original File
     ↓
Baseline / Hash
     ↓
File Modification
     ↓
Wazuh Detection
     ↓
Alert

This functionality can help identify:

Unauthorized file modifications;
Configuration changes;
Changes to critical files;
Potential indicators of compromise.

FIM is particularly relevant in defensive security because attackers may modify system files, configuration files, scripts, or other resources after gaining access to an endpoint.

17. Endpoint Inventory

The Wazuh inventory capabilities were also explored.

Inventory information provides visibility into the monitored endpoints, including information such as:

Operating system;
Hardware;
Installed software;
Packages;
System information.

This is important from a SOC perspective because an analyst needs to understand the asset involved in an event.

A security alert is much more useful when the analyst can determine:

What happened? Where did it happen? Which system was affected? What software is installed on that system?

18. Vulnerability Detection

The laboratory also explored Wazuh's Vulnerability Detection capabilities.

The feature provides information about vulnerabilities associated with software installed on monitored endpoints.

The general process can be represented as:

Endpoint
   ↓
Installed Software
   ↓
Software Version
   ↓
Known Vulnerability
   ↓
CVE
   ↓
Severity / Risk

This adds a vulnerability-management perspective to the SIEM environment.

Instead of only detecting security events, the platform can also provide information about weaknesses present in the monitored infrastructure.

19. MITRE ATT&CK Integration

The project also explored the relationship between Wazuh alerts and the MITRE ATT&CK framework.

MITRE ATT&CK provides a structured knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques.

The general relationship can be represented as:

Security Event
      ↓
Wazuh Rule
      ↓
Detection
      ↓
MITRE ATT&CK
      ↓
Tactic / Technique

This is important because a SOC analyst should not analyze events only in isolation.

Understanding the behavior represented by an event helps determine whether it may correspond to a known adversarial technique.

20. Generating Security Events

Once the environment was operational, controlled activities were performed to generate events.

One of the main tools used was Nmap.

Nmap was executed from Kali Linux to perform controlled network reconnaissance against the laboratory environment.

The purpose was not to compromise the systems, but to generate observable network activity and evaluate the monitoring capabilities of the environment.

The process was:

Kali Linux
     │
     │ Nmap
     ▼
Laboratory Target
     │
     ▼
Network Activity
     │
     ▼
Wazuh
     │
     ▼
Events / Alerts
     │
     ▼
Dashboard

This provided a practical way to validate whether the monitoring environment could observe security-related activity.

21. Investigating Events in the Dashboard

After generating the test activity, the Wazuh Dashboard was used to locate the corresponding events.

The investigation focused on information such as:

Timestamp;
Source endpoint;
Source IP address;
Event type;
Alert severity;
Rule ID;
Event description;
MITRE ATT&CK information when available.

This represents a simplified version of a typical SOC workflow:

Detect → Contextualize → Investigate → Classify

22. Event Filtering

Filtering events was another important part of the project.

A SIEM environment can receive a large volume of data.

Therefore, displaying every event simultaneously is not practical for an analyst.

The Wazuh Dashboard allows analysts to narrow their investigation using fields such as:

Agent;
IP address;
Rule;
Alert level;
Timestamp;
Event type;
Specific event fields.

This allowed us to isolate the events generated during our controlled tests.

23. Sysmon Troubleshooting

During the Windows configuration, we also encountered an issue where the Sysmon executable was not being recognized correctly.

The installation and execution path were verified, and the configuration was corrected.

After the necessary adjustments, Sysmon was functioning correctly and its events could be used as telemetry for the Wazuh environment.

This demonstrated another important troubleshooting principle:

Each component of a security monitoring pipeline should be tested independently before troubleshooting the SIEM itself.

