26. SOC Perspective

One of the main goals of the project was to simulate a simplified Security Operations Center.

A typical security monitoring workflow can be represented as:

          EVENT
            ↓
         COLLECTION
            ↓
        SIEM / SOC
            ↓
        DETECTION
            ↓
      INITIAL TRIAGE
            ↓
       INVESTIGATION
            ↓
      RISK CLASSIFICATION
            ↓
         RESPONSE
            ↓
        DOCUMENTATION

In our laboratory, Wazuh performed the main functions related to:

Telemetry collection;
Event processing;
Detection;
Alert generation;
Data storage;
Investigation support.

The analyst's responsibility is then to interpret the available information and determine whether an event represents legitimate activity, suspicious behavior, or a potential security incident.

27. Troubleshooting Performed

An important part of the project was not simply installing Wazuh, but troubleshooting the environment when components did not work as expected.

The main troubleshooting activities included:

Windows Agent

We investigated why the Windows agent was not initially appearing correctly in the Dashboard.

Sysmon

We corrected an issue involving the Sysmon executable and verified that Sysmon was properly installed and generating events.

Auditd

We verified the complete path from Auditd logs to the Wazuh Dashboard to ensure Linux audit events were being collected.

Network Connectivity

Connectivity between the endpoints and the Wazuh Server was verified to ensure that the agents could communicate correctly.

These troubleshooting activities were valuable because they simulated problems that can occur in real-world SOC and security infrastructure environments.

